WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.580
Imagine you own a watch that let's you travel through time.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.880
One day, you get the bright idea to travel back to the 1920's to mess with your grandparents.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.460
You press a few buttons, and suddenly you're standing beside old Pappy who is taking

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.780
in the scenery at the precipice of the Grand Canyon.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.940
Since you've never been the biggest fan of your grandfather, you decide to push him over the edge.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.580
Gravity does its thing, and a few seconds later, no more Pappy.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.260
But wait, if you killed your grandfather before he ever met your grandmother...

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.640
you wouldn't have been born to be able to go back in time and kill him!

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.280
Here's the problem. If you didn't exist and killed your grandfather, you couldn't have killed him...

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.780
Because you didn't kill him, he's still alive.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.180
but that would mean that you yourself would be alive and now you can go back in time to kill him.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.300
And so the endless loop goes on.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.280
This is called the Grandfather Paradox.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.940
And it's part of what makes time travel so interesting.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.720
The grandfather paradox deals with time travel on a significant scale.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.080
But why don't we start with something a little smaller?

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.120
Let's say you and I are wearing identical watches and we synchronize them right before I blast off into space.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.020
In the space shuttle, I'm traveling at roughly 28,000 kilometers per hour relative to the Earth.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.760
If I make a few orbits around the Earth before I return, when we compare watches,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.900
we'll see that less time has passed for me.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.820
A very, very tiny amount of time, but is there nonetheless.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.660
This is called time dilation, and it simply means that according to Einstein's theory of Relativity,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:24.980
Time measured along different trajectories is affected by differences in either gravity or velocity

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.320
– each of which affects time in different ways.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.340
One example of time dilation is when we compare the passage of time on the ISS versus on Earth.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.280
After spending six months on the Space Station, astronauts have aged approximately

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.580
.005 seconds less than those of us here on Earth.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.720
Of course, this is a very insignificant amount of time, but it would be much more apparent if the astronauts were

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.540
able to travel to closer to the speed of light.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.320
Interestingly, the effects of time dilation are fairly minor, even up to around 70% of the speed of light.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.500
However, once we reach 75% or so, the effects become dramatic.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.240
Now, bear with me here, because this is where things get a bit weird.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.760
Possibly the strangest aspect of special relativity is the distances shrink in the direction of motion.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.600
When we think of traveling to a point 10 light years away at 90% of light speed,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.040
you'd expect it to take 11 years, right?

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.920
That's not exactly the case.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.120
To a stationary observer, it would indeed 
seem to take your vessel 11 years.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.780
But to the people inside the vessel, 
not only time, but distance would dilate

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.840
and you would reach that  point in only 4.4 years.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.760
Want to play with time dilation yourself? I've added a link down below to a time dilation calculator.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.960
Okay, that's all really fascinating, but is it really time travel? Perhaps not in the sense that we usually

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.040
think of it, but according to the nature of
space-time, it is.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.760
If you were to return home from your 90% light speed trip, you would be almost 9 years older.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.760
But everyone back on Earth would have aged 22 years.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.880
So in essence, you've traveled 13 years into the future relative to your time in space.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.400
let's look at another example. In the film Interstellar, Cooper and his team land on a large watery planet,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.220
on which gravity is 30% stronger than on Earth.
This planet is situated very close to a

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:06.180
supermassive black hole called Gargantua, which it orbits at 55% of light.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.460
Gargantua's mass is equal to that of 100,000,000 suns and spins at 99.8% the speed of light.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.200
The combination of all these factors have the effect of slowing down time relative to the astronaut left aboard

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.540
the ship, by a staggering 61,000 times.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.300
That means that 1 hour on the planet is equal to seven years aboard the ship.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:31.040
When the team makes it back to the vessel after a little over three of their hours, their friend had aged by 23 years.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.740
The craziest part is that this isn't just science fiction. Those calculations all check out.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.280
That's exactly how it would happen in real life.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.140
So in essence, the crew had traveled 23 years into the future.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.460
Now so far, we've only explored traveling into the future.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.100
Is it possible to travel backwards in time?

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.680
According to some theories, specific types of motion in space might allow time travel into the past and future,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.020
if these geometries and motions were possible.

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.080
However, that's a big if.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.220
It would require something called a closed timelike curve, or CTC for short.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:08.260
A CTC is a closed loop in space-time which could theoretically allow an object to return to its own past.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.040
The science behind time travel to the past,
is incredibly complex and speculative

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.420
and many scientists suspect that it is not possible at all,
because of the issue of causality.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.060
That bring us back to the Grandfather Paradox,

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:23.360
if in fact these scientists are correct and assuming traveling to the past would cause a paradox

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.520
then we have our answer, time travel to the past is impossible.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.280
If however, the Novikov Self-Consistency Principle is correct, then there is still a chance.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.800
The Novikov Principle states that if an event exists that would cause a paradox or

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.860
any change to the past whatsoever, then the probability of that event is zero.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.760
It would thus be impossible to create time paradoxes.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.520
Unfortunately, for any would-be time travelers, the Novikov Principle is not widely accepted.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.960
Another possibility would be the existence of wormholes, which are technically

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.920
permitted by general relativity.
- In order to travel through time using a wormhole,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.880
it would have to be what is known as a
Transversable Wormhole.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.780
Brace yourself for more strange 
space-time shenanigans here.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.120
In order to be able to travel through time using a Transversable Wormhole,

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.520
its creation would have to be in one of two ways.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:11.100
Option one, one end of the wormhole would have to be accelerated to a significant portion of the speed of light,

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.800
then brought back to the origin point.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.500
time dilation would result in an accelerated wormhole entrance

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.660
aging less than the stationary one as seen by an external observer

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.360
Option 2 requires one end of the wormhole to be placed

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.700
within the gravitational field of an object with higher gravity than the other entrrance

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.120
and then returned to a position near the other entrance

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.280
This is a difficult concept to grasp

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.540
So think of it this way,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.560
imagine you and I synchronize our watches to both display the year 2000

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.840
I hop in my ship and accelerate one entrance to the wormhole to near light speed

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.060
and then bring it back

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.860
My watch now reads 2004

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.220
And yours reads 2012

00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:45.580
If someone were then to enter the accelerated entrance

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.140
they would now exit the stationary entrance in the year 2004

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:52.360
the same location, but 8 years in their past

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.780
It's incredibly complicated stuff

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.380
But an easier way to look at it

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:57.680
is by picturing by a piece of paper

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.300
This paper represent space time,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.860
a wormhole is an area of warped spacetime with an entrance and an exit

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.640
which you can imagine as the paper being folded back on itself

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.680
with a hole poked through it

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.960
essentially you're simply skipping all the time and distance between the two points

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:14.280
The problem with transversable wormholes

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:18.400
is that you can't possibly travel back further than the initial creation of the wormhole

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.820
So...really it's more a path through time rather than

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.520
a device propels itself back and forth through the years at will

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:26.920
It could be useful for the people in the distant future

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.340
wanting to come observe our time

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:30.280
but if we created such a portal today

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.160
we couldn't use it to go visit the dinosaurs

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.460
One final method that could potentially allow an

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.940
individual to more or less travel through time

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:38.160
is cryopreservation

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.520
The branch of science concerned specifically

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.520
with preserving human is called cryonics

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.280
and the practice has been around since the late 1960s.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.860
Cryonics is on the speculative edge of medicine

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:49.140
as its proponents suggest that

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.720
Death is not a singular event, but a process

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.840
it would have to be for the practice to be successful

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.000
because it's currently illegal for a human to be frozen before they die

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.620
One a person opted to be cryo-preserved

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.740
their bodies prepared minutes after official death

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.760
the theory is that by reducing the patient's body temperature

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.140
to around -130 degrees celcius

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.800
enough brain information will be retained in an accessible state

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.880
for doctors and scientists of the far future to revive the patient

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.740
temperatures of that low inevitably cause significant damage to the human body

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.380
regardless of prior-preservation safeguards

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.640
so the doctors of today are relying on the development of

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.980
future technology that will allow the brain to be repaired at the molecular level

